\begin{abstract}
We study \emph{fading ladders}: sequences $\rho_0,\rho_1,\rho_2,\dots$ of measured association
strengths recorded on a regular grid of a complexity parameter. The motivating instance is the
Spearman rank correlation between the trailing-zero statistic $T(x)=\nu_2(x)$ of a uniformly drawn
$b$-bit integer and a downstream response, measured on the four-bit grid $b=96,100,\dots,120$, with
pooled reading $\rho(104)=0.500$, confidence interval $[0.456,0.545]$, and per-seed values
$0.493/0.499/0.509$. We prove: (i) an exact dichotomy --- every fade with nonnegative decrements
either has a uniform lower bound or reaches $0$ at a finite rung, the criterion being
\emph{summability} of the decrements rather than their decay; (ii) that vanishing decrements
certify nothing --- the harmonic fade with the recorded start $0.5739$ and first step $0.0303$ has
decrements decreasing to $0$, stays above $0.33$ throughout its first $128$ four-bit steps (a
span of $512$ bits, four times the entire recorded sweep), and is
extinguished by its $2^{36}$-th step; (iii) non-identifiability --- for any observed ladder
$g_0,\dots,g_N$ with $g_N>0$ two antitone continuations reproduce all observations exactly while
one is bounded below by $g_N/2>0$ and the other equals $0$ at rung $N+10$, so that a floor at
$0.21818$ and extinction at $b=160$ are both consistent with all seven recorded rungs; and (iv)
that contraction is exactly the missing hypothesis and the data violate it --- a $q$-contractive
ladder ($|d_{k+1}|\le q|d_k|$, $0\le q<1$) satisfies the tail bound
$|\rho_{n+m}-\rho_n|\le |d_n|/(1-q)$, hence has the explicit floor $\rho_n-|d_n|/(1-q)$ and is never
extinguished once that floor is positive, whereas the recorded ladder admits no contraction factor
below $2$, the rebound $-0.0226$ at $b=116$ being retraced at $b=120$ by a step of $+0.04834$. We
add a curvature obstruction: exact second-difference identities show every convex hyperbolic or
geometric law decelerates while the recorded decrements accelerate, and the full ladder contains
both a strictly convex and a strictly concave grid triple, excluding every fixed-curvature-sign
law. We give auditing and certification algorithms and specify a discriminating measurement at
$b=156$, where the two extremal continuations differ by more than $0.17$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} fading ladder, contraction factor, tail bound, non-identifiability,
$2$-adic valuation, Spearman correlation, feature degradation, extrapolation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

Let $x$ be drawn uniformly from the $b$-bit integers and let $T(x)=\nu_2(x)$ be the number of
trailing zeros in its binary expansion, i.e.\ the $2$-adic valuation. In the pipeline under study
$T$ serves as a predictive feature for a downstream scalar response, and its quality is summarised
by the Spearman rank correlation
\[
  \rho(b) \;=\; \operatorname{corr}_{\mathrm{rank}}\bigl(T,\ \mathrm{rate}\bigr)
\]
estimated from a large uniform sample at bit-length $b$. As $b$ grows, $\rho(b)$ decays; we call
this \emph{the fade}.

The current cycle records $b=104$ across three seeds.

